    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p46-q70-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Since $f(x)$ is monic, it can be expressed as\n$$f(x)=(x-\\alpha)(x-\\beta)(x-\\gamma).$$\nShow that $f'(\\alpha)f'(\\beta)f'(\\gamma)=-\\Delta(f)$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f'(\\alpha)f'(\\beta)f'(\\gamma)=-(\\alpha-\\beta)^2(\\alpha-\\gamma)^2(\\beta-\\gamma)^2=-\\Delta(f)$.\n\nDifferentiate the factored form using the product rule, evaluate at each root and multiply.",
          "worked": "Using the product rule,\n$$f'(x)=(x-\\beta)(x-\\gamma)+(x-\\alpha)(x-\\gamma)+(x-\\alpha)(x-\\beta).$$\nThus,\n$$\\begin{align*}f'(\\alpha)&=(\\alpha-\\beta)(\\alpha-\\gamma),\\\\f'(\\beta)&=(\\beta-\\alpha)(\\beta-\\gamma),\\\\f'(\\gamma)&=(\\gamma-\\alpha)(\\gamma-\\beta).\\end{align*}$$\nEach reversed difference contributes a negative sign. Therefore,\n$$\\begin{align*}f'(\\alpha)f'(\\beta)f'(\\gamma)&=[(\\alpha-\\beta)(\\beta-\\alpha)][(\\alpha-\\gamma)(\\gamma-\\alpha)][(\\beta-\\gamma)(\\gamma-\\beta)]\\\\f'(\\alpha)f'(\\beta)f'(\\gamma)&=-(\\alpha-\\beta)^2(\\alpha-\\gamma)^2(\\beta-\\gamma)^2\\\\f'(\\alpha)f'(\\beta)f'(\\gamma)&=-\\Delta(f).\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 84
      },
      {
        "id": "p46-q70-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence, show that if $f(x)=x^3+px+q$, then\n$$\\Delta(f)=-27q^2-4p^3.$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\Delta(f)=-27q^2-4p^3$.\n\nExpand $(3\\alpha^2+p)(3\\beta^2+p)(3\\gamma^2+p)$ and simplify using the cubic root-coefficient relationships.",
          "worked": "The cubic root-coefficient relationships give\n$$\\begin{align*}\\alpha+\\beta+\\gamma&=0,\\\\\\alpha\\beta+\\alpha\\gamma+\\beta\\gamma&=p,\\\\\\alpha\\beta\\gamma&=-q.\\end{align*}$$\nHence,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\alpha^2+\\beta^2+\\gamma^2&=(\\alpha+\\beta+\\gamma)^2-2(\\alpha\\beta+\\alpha\\gamma+\\beta\\gamma)\\\\\\alpha^2+\\beta^2+\\gamma^2&=-2p,\\\\\\alpha^2\\beta^2+\\alpha^2\\gamma^2+\\beta^2\\gamma^2&=(\\alpha\\beta+\\alpha\\gamma+\\beta\\gamma)^2-2\\alpha\\beta\\gamma(\\alpha+\\beta+\\gamma)\\\\\\alpha^2\\beta^2+\\alpha^2\\gamma^2+\\beta^2\\gamma^2&=p^2.\\end{align*}$$\nSince $f'(x)=3x^2+p$,\n$$\\begin{align*}f'(\\alpha)f'(\\beta)f'(\\gamma)&=(3\\alpha^2+p)(3\\beta^2+p)(3\\gamma^2+p)\\\\f'(\\alpha)f'(\\beta)f'(\\gamma)&=27\\alpha^2\\beta^2\\gamma^2+9p(\\alpha^2\\beta^2+\\alpha^2\\gamma^2+\\beta^2\\gamma^2)+3p^2(\\alpha^2+\\beta^2+\\gamma^2)+p^3\\\\f'(\\alpha)f'(\\beta)f'(\\gamma)&=27q^2+9p(p^2)+3p^2(-2p)+p^3\\\\f'(\\alpha)f'(\\beta)f'(\\gamma)&=27q^2+4p^3.\\end{align*}$$\nBy part (a),\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Delta(f)&=-f'(\\alpha)f'(\\beta)f'(\\gamma)\\\\\\Delta(f)&=-27q^2-4p^3.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 140
      }
    ]
  },
